{"primaryContentSections":[{"kind":"declarations","declarations":[{"tokens":[{"kind":"keyword","text":"let"},{"text":" ","kind":"text"},{"text":"ending","kind":"identifier"},{"text":": ","kind":"text"},{"identifier":"doc:\/\/IvorMXL\/documentation\/IvorMXL\/MXLEnding","text":"MXLEnding","kind":"typeIdentifier","preciseIdentifier":"s:7IvorMXL9MXLEndingV"},{"text":"?","kind":"text"}],"platforms":["macOS"],"languages":["swift"]}]},{"kind":"content","content":[{"text":"Discussion","type":"heading","anchor":"discussion","level":2},{"type":"paragraph","inlineContent":[{"text":"Maps to a nested MusicXML ","type":"text"},{"type":"codeVoice","code":"<ending>"},{"text":" element.","type":"text"}]}]}],"schemaVersion":{"minor":3,"patch":0,"major":0},"hierarchy":{"paths":[["doc:\/\/IvorMXL\/documentation\/IvorMXL","doc:\/\/IvorMXL\/documentation\/IvorMXL\/MXLBarline"]]},"sections":[],"abstract":[{"type":"text","text":"The multiple-ending bracket associated with this barline."}],"variants":[{"paths":["\/documentation\/ivormxl\/mxlbarline\/ending"],"traits":[{"interfaceLanguage":"swift"}]}],"kind":"symbol","identifier":{"url":"doc:\/\/IvorMXL\/documentation\/IvorMXL\/MXLBarline\/ending","interfaceLanguage":"swift"},"metadata":{"roleHeading":"Instance Property","modules":[{"name":"IvorMXL"}],"symbolKind":"property","role":"symbol","fragments":[{"text":"let","kind":"keyword"},{"text":" ","kind":"text"},{"text":"ending","kind":"identifier"},{"text":": ","kind":"text"},{"text":"MXLEnding","kind":"typeIdentifier","preciseIdentifier":"s:7IvorMXL9MXLEndingV"},{"text":"?","kind":"text"}],"title":"ending","externalID":"s:7IvorMXL10MXLBarlineV6endingAA9MXLEndingVSgvp"},"references":{"doc://IvorMXL/documentation/IvorMXL":{"url":"\/documentation\/ivormxl","kind":"symbol","type":"topic","role":"collection","title":"IvorMXL","identifier":"doc:\/\/IvorMXL\/documentation\/IvorMXL","abstract":[{"type":"text","text":"A MusicXML parser, normalizer, validator, and formatter."}]},"doc://IvorMXL/documentation/IvorMXL/MXLBarline":{"fragments":[{"kind":"keyword","text":"struct"},{"kind":"text","text":" "},{"kind":"identifier","text":"MXLBarline"}],"kind":"symbol","type":"topic","identifier":"doc:\/\/IvorMXL\/documentation\/IvorMXL\/MXLBarline","role":"symbol","navigatorTitle":[{"kind":"identifier","text":"MXLBarline"}],"title":"MXLBarline","abstract":[{"type":"text","text":"If a barline is other than a normal single barline, it should be represented by a barline type that describes"},{"type":"text","text":" "},{"type":"text","text":"it. This includes information about repeats and multiple endings, as well as line style. Barline data is on"},{"type":"text","text":" "},{"type":"text","text":"the same level as the other musical data in a score — a child of a measure in a partwise score, or a part in a"},{"type":"text","text":" "},{"type":"text","text":"timewise score. This allows for barlines within measures, as in dotted barlines that subdivide measures in"},{"type":"text","text":" "},{"type":"text","text":"complex meters. The two fermata elements allow for fermatas on both sides of the barline (the lower one"},{"type":"text","text":" "},{"type":"text","text":"inverted)."}],"url":"\/documentation\/ivormxl\/mxlbarline"},"doc://IvorMXL/documentation/IvorMXL/MXLBarline/ending":{"title":"ending","role":"symbol","identifier":"doc:\/\/IvorMXL\/documentation\/IvorMXL\/MXLBarline\/ending","type":"topic","abstract":[{"text":"The multiple-ending bracket associated with this barline.","type":"text"}],"fragments":[{"kind":"keyword","text":"let"},{"kind":"text","text":" "},{"kind":"identifier","text":"ending"},{"kind":"text","text":": "},{"kind":"typeIdentifier","text":"MXLEnding","preciseIdentifier":"s:7IvorMXL9MXLEndingV"},{"kind":"text","text":"?"}],"kind":"symbol","url":"\/documentation\/ivormxl\/mxlbarline\/ending"},"doc://IvorMXL/documentation/IvorMXL/MXLEnding":{"identifier":"doc:\/\/IvorMXL\/documentation\/IvorMXL\/MXLEnding","role":"symbol","navigatorTitle":[{"text":"MXLEnding","kind":"identifier"}],"type":"topic","kind":"symbol","fragments":[{"text":"struct","kind":"keyword"},{"text":" ","kind":"text"},{"text":"MXLEnding","kind":"identifier"}],"abstract":[{"type":"text","text":"The ending type represents multiple (e.g. first and second) endings. Typically, the start type is associated"},{"type":"text","text":" "},{"type":"text","text":"with the left barline of the first measure in an ending. The stop and discontinue types are associated with"},{"type":"text","text":" "},{"type":"text","text":"the right barline of the last measure in an ending. Stop is used when the ending mark concludes with a"},{"type":"text","text":" "},{"type":"text","text":"downward jog, as is typical for first endings. Discontinue is used when there is no downward jog, as is"},{"type":"text","text":" "},{"type":"text","text":"typical for second endings that do not conclude a piece. The length of the jog can be specified using the end-"},{"type":"text","text":" "},{"type":"text","text":"length attribute. The text-x and text-y attributes are offsets that specify where the baseline of the start of"},{"type":"text","text":" "},{"type":"text","text":"the ending text appears, relative to the start of the ending line."}],"url":"\/documentation\/ivormxl\/mxlending","title":"MXLEnding"}}}